  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nOther than passing high clouds and elevated wildfire smoke, \nmainly clear skies are displayed on satellite imagery this \nmorning. Patchy fog is showing up just south of the IA/MN border\nand into far SE MN, but fog is not expected to be an issue for \nthe WFO MPX coverage area this morning. What is of concern is \nthe approaching cold front currently draped over NW MN, \nemanating from a 999mb surface low south of Hudson Bay. This \nsystem is underneath a broad central NOAM trough, which is \nforecast to shove a digging axis around the base of the broad \ntrough, sweeping through the Dakotas and Upper Midwest later \ntoday. Not a whole lot of moisture is associated with the \nanticipated frontal passage later today, thus limiting overall \ninstability to potentially 1000-1500 j/kg of MUCAPE. That said, \nbulk shear of around 35kt pushing through the area in \nconjunction with the frontal dynamics, along with favorable \nearly-mid afternoon diurnal timing, will allow for isolated to \nscattered thunderstorm development this afternoon. The focus \nareas look to be far southern and eastern MN into western WI. \nShould lapse rates be realized in the 7-8 deg C/km rates, as has\nbeen advertised the past 24-48hrs for today, then along with \nthe mid-level stronger winds, a few storms could produce large \nhail and damaging winds. SPC maintains the Marginal (Level 1 of \n5) risk for eastern portions of our coverage area so the bottom \nline is that there is a non-zero risk for severe thunderstorms \nthis afternoon, even despite the overall smaller PoPs and meager\nexpected coverage of storms today.\n\nThe cold front will slide into WI by this evening then into the \ncentral Great Lakes tonight, allowing for any and all precip to \nbe out of our coverage area by sunset. High pressure will then \nprevail tonight through all day Saturday. A developing front \nover northern MN and the Dakotas looks to slide southward \nthrough our area Saturday night, linking up with a low pressure \nsystem shifting eastward from the Plains into the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley Saturday night. Additional \nshowers/thunderstorms look likely near the I-90 corridor portion\nof MN Saturday night into early Sunday morning. A few \nthunderstorms are possible but the better storm coverage and \nstrength will be in IA and points south. Similarly, another \nround of showers/thunderstorms looks likely for far southern \nportions of our coverage area Sunday night into Monday morning \nalong the same stalled frontal boundary. Again, better storm \ncoverage/strength will be south and southeast of the WFO MPX \ncoverage area, and both these systems will also have very little\npotential for heavy rain since the dynamics and frontal forcing\nwill be better to the south of our area.\n\nOnce past these weekend chances of rain, a mainly dry period is \nexpected for much of next week as systems develop over the \nRockies but will have little impetus to reach our portion of the\nlower 48, and systems look to phase over the Ohio Valley and \nbring precipitation to the Appalachians and points east. Thus, \nthis scenario will leave us primarily high and dry for most of \nnext week. Little in the way or airmass change from now until \nthe middle of next week will mean that elevated wildfire smoke \nwill still be possible, but this will have little effect to the \nsurface other than keeping temperatures from getting far away \nfrom normal values. Highs will remain in the upper 70s to mid \n80s from the end of this week into the middle of next week. \nFinally by the end of next week, a more pronounced eastern NOAM \ntrough with a Four Corners ridge pattern looks to develop, which\nwill make for solid NW flow aloft for the Upper Midwest. This \nwill make a pathway for cooler central Canadian air to spread \nsouthward into our area, thus dropping highs back to the 70s \narea-wide and lows to the 50s area-wide for the latter portion \nof next week.",
